Fixtures for test/embedding_check
=================================

Each fixture is a `.crd` file: three whitespace-separated columns per vertex, a
blank line between components, every component implicitly closed (last vertex
back to first). This is exactly the format `LinkEmbedding*::FromFile`
accepts and the format `ndmansfield` emits, so the files are fed to both the
library reader and the test's own parser and the two are checked against each
other (the `reader` tier).

The extension is `.crd` and not `.tsv` on purpose: `.gitattributes` routes all
`*.tsv` through git-lfs, and these files are small enough that LFS would be pure
overhead.

Every fixture may have a `.expect` sidecar. Those are read only by the test, so
they may contain `#` comments; the `.crd` files must not, because the library
reader does not accept them.

`.expect` syntax
----------------

    <census-key> ==  n        exact count required
    <census-key> >=  n        lower bound required
    knot   = ...              documentation only
    note   = ...              documentation only
    xfail_<tier>  = [classes |] reason
    xcrash_<tier> = [classes |] reason

Census keys are the fields of `Census` in ../embedding_fixtures.hpp:
`zero_length_edges`, `vertical_edges`, `coincident_vertices`, `vertex_on_edge`,
`corner_corner`, `collinear_overlap`, `multiple_points`,
`max_point_multiplicity`, `transversal`, `spatial`, `degenerate_total`,
`vertices`, `edges`.

The census is computed independently of the classes under test, in exact integer
arithmetic. Its purpose is to make "we exercised the corner cases" checkable: if
a fixture silently stops being degenerate, the declared counts stop matching and
the test says so. `spatial == 0` is declared on every fixture -- a genuine 3D
intersection is the one degeneracy no symbolic perturbation can repair, so a
fixture containing one would make a correct error return look like a failure.

`xfail_<tier>` marks a known wrong answer, `xcrash_<tier>` a known crash. Both
take an optional comma-separated class list before a `|`, so a defect can be
scoped to the classes that actually have it. Both are checked in both
directions: if a marked check starts passing, that is reported as an XPASS
failure so the stale marker gets removed rather than masking a later regression.

Hand-built fixtures (`deg_*`)
-----------------------------

One degeneracy each, small enough to read, with the intended configuration
spelled out in the `.expect` file:

  deg_corner_corner       two vertices project onto each other (4 edges meet)
  deg_vertex_on_edge      a vertex projects into a segment's interior
  deg_vertical_edge       an edge projects to a point, on another edge
  deg_stacked_verticals   6 edges through one projected point, 2 of them vertical
  deg_collinear_overlap   two non-adjacent edges sharing a projected segment
  deg_zero_length         a zero-length edge, plus a corner-corner
  deg_triple_point        three non-adjacent segments concurrent at one point
  deg_hopf_flat           2-component link; one component projects to a segment

`deg_hopf_flat` is the one whose type is fixed by construction rather than by
computation: component 1's vertical edge at (2,2) pierces the disk bounded by
the square component 0 exactly once, so it is a Hopf link.

Lattice fixtures (`lattice_NN`)
-------------------------------

Closed Hamiltonian cycles on the NN^3 cubic lattice -- space-filling knots with
unit-length edges, which present every degeneracy class at once and in bulk
(lattice_16 has a projected point with 30 edges through it). These are the
motivating input for the exact-integer backends and the material for `--bench`.

Generated with the `fastermansfield` fork of ndmansfield
(https://github.com/jewettaij/ndmansfield), whose `-record-loops` mode collects
Hamiltonian *paths* whose two ends sit one lattice unit apart; closing that last
bond turns each into an exact lattice knot. The generator is not a build
dependency -- the outputs are committed -- but the exact commands were:

    ndmansfield -box  4  4  4 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 1  ->  lattice_04
    ndmansfield -box  6  6  6 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 3  ->  lattice_06
    ndmansfield -box  8  8  8 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 4  ->  lattice_08
    ndmansfield -box 10 10 10 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 7  ->  lattice_10
    ndmansfield -box 12 12 12 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 5  ->  lattice_12
    ndmansfield -box 16 16 16 -record-loops -loop-count 1 -loop-dir D -seed 6  ->  lattice_16

each followed by `loop_0000000.tsv` -> `lattice_NN.crd` verbatim. Before being
committed, each was checked to have exactly NN^3 vertices, every consecutive
pair one unit apart, and the two ends one unit apart (so the closing bond is a
genuine lattice edge).

Only even box sizes appear: a k^3 grid graph is bipartite with colour classes of
sizes ceil(k^3/2) and floor(k^3/2), so for odd k^3 the two differ and no
Hamiltonian *cycle* exists -- `-record-loops` simply never finds one.

The knot types are not known a priori, and that is fine: the test does not need
them. It compares the degenerate projection against explicit generic ones of the
same curve, which is self-validating.
